{"batchcomplete":true,"query":{"pages":[{"pageid":33319665,"ns":0,"title":"Dilepton","revisions":[{"revid":1373742336,"parentid":1373742234,"minor":false,"timestamp":"2026-09-07T17:58:35Z","size":10862,"sha1":"9212474789497faf5fa8e42e3a6e6f2bca0bb4f8","slots":{"main":{"contentmodel":"wikitext","contentformat":"text/x-wiki","content":"{{Short description|Subatomic particles}}\nA '''dilepton''' or '''lepton pair''' is a [[lepton]]-[[antilepton]] pair. They can be emitted during heavy-ion collisions.<ref>{{cite journal|last1=Bailhache|first1=R.|last2=Appelshäuser|first2=H.|date=September 22, 2025|title=Dileptons at Colliders as Probes of the Quark–Gluon Plasma|url=https://www.annualreviews.org/content/journals/10.1146/annurev-nucl-121423-100858|journal=Annual Review of Nuclear and Particle Science|language=en|volume=75|issue=1|pages=463–486|doi=10.1146/annurev-nucl-121423-100858|issn=0163-8998|arxiv=2512.10597|bibcode=2025ARNPS..75..463B }}</ref> In the [[parton model]], these pairs result from the [[Drell–Yan process]].<ref>{{cite journal|last1=McGaughey|first1=P. L.|last2=Moss|first2=J. M.|last3=Peng|first3=J. C.|date=December 1, 1999|title=HIGH-ENERGY HADRON-INDUCED DILEPTON PRODUCTION FROM NUCLEONS AND NUCLEI|url=https://www.annualreviews.org/content/journals/10.1146/annurev.nucl.49.1.217|journal=Annual Review of Nuclear and Particle Science|language=en|volume=49|pages=217–253|doi=10.1146/annurev.nucl.49.1.217|issn=0163-8998|arxiv=hep-ph/9905409|bibcode=1999ARNPS..49..217M}}</ref>\n\n==Experimental advantages==\nPhotons are also created in heavy-ion collisions, but a key advantage of thermal dileptons over real photons is the presence of an additional kinematic variable: the invariant mass. While real photons are characterized only by their transverse momentum (''p''<sub>T</sub>), lepton pairs are described by both invariant mass (''M'') and transverse momentum. For thermal radiation, transverse-momentum spectra are sensitive to a combination of temperature and collective expansion velocity (radial flow), which complicates their interpretation. By contrast, the invariant mass is Lorentz invariant and largely insensitive to flow effects, making the dilepton mass spectrum a particularly clean and largely model-independent observable for extracting the true thermal temperature of the emitting medium.\n\nIn the intermediate-mass region (IMR), with invariant mass above about 1&nbsp;GeV, hadronic resonance contributions become small and the electromagnetic spectral function is expected to be approximately flat, as in the black-body case. In this regime, thermal dilepton emission exhibits a nearly exponential, Planck-like mass spectrum, providing direct access to the average temperature of the emitting medium and sensitivity to radiation from the deconfined QGP phase.\n\n==Experimental evidence==\n===CERES experiment===\nThe first experimental evidence for thermal dilepton production in relativistic heavy-ion collisions was obtained by the [[CERES experiment|CERES]] experiment at the [[Super Proton Synchrotron|CERN Super Proton Synchrotron (SPS)]], which observed an enhancement of low-mass lepton pairs above known hadronic decay sources.<ref>{{cite journal\n | last1 = Agakichiev\n | first1 = G.\n | collaboration = CERES\n | title = Enhanced production of low mass electron pairs in 200 GeV/u S–Au collisions at the CERN SPS\n | journal = Physical Review Letters\n | volume = 75\n | pages = 1272–1275\n | year = 1995\n | issue = 7\n | doi = 10.1103/PhysRevLett.75.1272\n| pmid = 10060251\n }}</ref><ref>{{cite journal\n | author = ((G. Agakichiev et al. (CERES/NA45 Collaboration)))\n | title = Low-mass e<sup>+</sup>e<sup>−</sup> pair production in 158 AGeV Pb–Au collisions at the CERN SPS and its dependence on multiplicity and transverse momentum\n | journal = Physics Letters B\n | volume = 422\n | pages = 405–412\n | year = 1998\n | doi = 10.1016/S0370-2693(98)00083-5\n | arxiv = nucl-ex/9712008\n}}</ref><ref>{{cite journal\n | author = B. Lenkeit et al. (CERES Collaboration)\n | title = Recent results from Pb–Au collisions at 158 GeV/c per nucleon obtained with the CERES spectrometer\n | journal = Nuclear Physics A\n | volume = 661\n | pages = 23–32\n | year = 1999\n | issue = 1–4\n | doi = 10.1016/S0375-9474(99)85005-2\n | arxiv = nucl-ex/9910015\n| bibcode = 1999NuPhA.661...23L\n }}</ref><ref>{{cite journal\n | author = G. Agakichiev et al. (CERES Collaboration)\n | title = e<sup>+</sup>e<sup>−</sup> pair production in Pb–Au collisions at 158 GeV per nucleon\n | journal = European Physical Journal C\n | volume = 41\n | pages = 475–513\n | year = 2005\n | issue = 4\n | doi = 10.1140/epjc/s2005-02272-3\n  | arxiv = nucl-ex/0506002\n}}</ref><ref>{{cite journal\n | author = ((D. Adamova et al. (CERES/NA45 Collaboration)))\n | title = Enhanced production of low mass electron pairs in 40 AGeV Pb–Au collisions at the CERN SPS\n | journal = Physical Review Letters\n | volume = 91\n | article-number = 042301\n | year = 2003\n | issue = 4\n | doi = 10.1103/PhysRevLett.91.042301\n | pmid = 12906652\n | arxiv = nucl-ex/0209024\n}}</ref>\nThese measurements established dileptons as sensitive probes of hot and dense strongly interacting matter and of in-medium modifications of vector mesons.\n\n===NA60 experiment===\nA major advance was achieved by the [[NA60 experiment|NA60]] experiment at the SPS, which measured thermal dilepton radiation with unprecedented mass resolution and statistical precision for heavy-ion collisions.\n\n====Low-mass region (LMR)====\nIn the low-mass region (''M'' &lt; 1&nbsp;GeV), NA60 demonstrated that the dilepton excess is dominated by hadronic radiation, primarily π<sup>+</sup>π<sup>−</sup> annihilation mediated by a strongly broadened in-medium ρ meson. The measurements provided direct experimental access to the in-medium ρ spectral function and resolved a long-standing controversy regarding its modifications in hot and dense matter. The observed strong broadening, without a significant mass shift, revealed the mechanism related to chiral symmetry restoration near the [[Quantum chromodynamics|QCD]] phase boundary, offering experimental insight into how hadron properties are modified as chiral symmetry is restored in the medium. In addition, the data exhibited clear signatures of collective radial flow, linking the dilepton signal to the dynamics of the expanding fireball and to the evolution of the strongly interacting medium.\n\n====Intermediate-mass region (IMR)====\nIn the intermediate-mass region (''M'' ≳ 1.1&nbsp;GeV), NA60 observed a nearly exponential mass spectrum consistent with thermal radiation from a source with a flat electromagnetic spectral function. Fits to the IMR mass spectrum yielded temperatures exceeding 200&nbsp;MeV (e.g. ''T'' = 205&nbsp;±&nbsp;12&nbsp;MeV for 1.1&nbsp;&lt; ''M'' &lt; 2.0&nbsp;GeV and ''T'' = 230&nbsp;±&nbsp;10&nbsp;MeV for 1.1&nbsp;&lt; ''M'' &lt; 2.4&nbsp;GeV), well above the QCD critical temperature ''T''<sub>c</sub> of 155 MeV as determined by lattice QCD. Because invariant mass is Lorentz invariant, these results provide a direct, flow-independent measurement of the medium temperature.\n\nIndependent analyses of transverse-mass spectra revealed a characteristic change around ''M'' = 1&nbsp;GeV: the effective slope parameter increases with mass in the LMR, consistent with hadronic emission boosted by radial flow, but drops and becomes approximately constant in the IMR, with ''T''<sub>eff</sub> values around 200&nbsp;MeV. This behaviour indicated early emission with comparatively little collective flow (i.e. little or no blue shift). NA60 also measured the angular distributions of the excess dileptons and found them to be consistent with isotropy, with vanishing polarization coefficients, supporting an interpretation of the excess as thermal radiation from a randomized medium rather than from strongly directed annihilation processes.<ref>{{cite journal\n |last=Arnaldi\n |first=R.\n |collaboration=NA60 Collaboration\n |title=First measurement of the rho spectral function in high-energy nuclear collisions\n |journal=Physical Review Letters\n |volume=96\n |article-number=162302\n |year=2006\n |issue=16\n |doi=10.1103/PhysRevLett.96.162302\n|pmid=16712218\n |arxiv=nucl-ex/0605007\n |bibcode=2006PhRvL..96p2302A\n }}</ref><ref>{{cite journal\n |last=Arnaldi\n |first=R.\n |collaboration=NA60 Collaboration\n |title=Evidence for radial flow of thermal dileptons in high-energy nuclear collisions\n |journal=Physical Review Letters\n |volume=100\n |article-number=022302\n |year=2008\n |issue=2\n |doi=10.1103/PhysRevLett.100.022302\n|pmid=18232858\n |arxiv=0711.1816\n |bibcode=2008PhRvL.100b2302A\n }}</ref><ref>{{cite journal\n |last=Arnaldi\n |first=R.\n |collaboration=NA60 Collaboration\n |title=Evidence for the production of thermal-like muon pairs with masses above 1&nbsp;GeV/c{{sup|2}} in 158&nbsp;A&nbsp;GeV indium–indium collisions\n |journal=The European Physical Journal C\n |volume=59\n |pages=607–623\n |year=2009\n |doi=10.1140/epjc/s10052-008-0857-2\n |doi-access=free|arxiv=0810.3204\n }}</ref><ref>{{cite journal\n |last=Arnaldi\n |first=R.\n |collaboration=NA60 Collaboration\n |title=First results on angular distributions of thermal dileptons in nuclear collisions\n |journal=Physical Review Letters\n |volume=102\n |article-number=222301\n |year=2009\n |issue=22\n |doi=10.1103/PhysRevLett.102.222301\n|pmid=19658858\n |arxiv=0812.3100\n |bibcode=2009PhRvL.102v2301A\n }}</ref><ref>{{cite magazine\n |last=Damjanovic\n |first=Sanja\n |author2=Ruben Shahoyan\n |author3=Hans J. Specht\n |collaboration=NA60 Collaboration\n |title=NA60: In hot pursuit of thermal dileptons\n |magazine=CERN Courier\n |volume=49\n |issue=11\n |pages=31–34\n |year=2009\n |url=https://cerncourier.com/a/na60-in-hot-pursuit-of-thermal-dileptons/\n |publisher=CERN\n}}</ref><ref>{{cite journal\n | author = ((Hans J. Specht (for the NA60 Collaboration)))\n | title = Thermal dileptons from hot and dense strongly interacting matter\n | journal = AIP Conference Proceedings\n | volume = 1322\n | issue = 1\n | pages = 1–10\n | year = 2010\n | doi = 10.1063/1.3541982\n| arxiv = 1011.0615\n | bibcode = 2010AIPC.1322....1S\n }}</ref>\n\n==Significance==\nTaken together, the measured dilepton mass spectra, transverse-momentum distributions, and angular distributions provide a largely model-independent signature of thermal radiation from the hot and dense medium created in heavy-ion collisions and establish thermal dileptons as an experimental thermometer for quark–gluon matter.<ref>{{cite book\n |last=Damjanovic\n |first=Sanja\n |author2=Volker Metag\n |author3=Jürgen Schukraft\n |editor-last=Damjanovic\n |editor-first=Sanja\n |editor2-last=Metag\n |editor2-first=Volker\n |editor3-last=Schukraft\n |editor3-first=Jürgen\n |chapter=Research at the CERN accelerators from 1983 onwards – ultra-relativistic heavy-ion physics: searching for the quark–gluon plasma\n |title=Hans Joachim Specht – Scientist and Visionary\n |series=Springer Biographies\n |publisher=Springer Nature\n |location=Cham\n |pages=58–93\n |year=2024\n |doi=10.1007/978-3-031-92353-1\n|isbn=978-3-031-92352-4\n }}</ref>\n\n==See also==\n* [[Pair production]]\n\n==References==\n<references/>\n\n[[Category:Antimatter]]\n[[Category:Leptons]]"}}}],"contentmodel":"wikitext","pagelanguage":"en","pagelanguagehtmlcode":"en","pagelanguagedir":"ltr","touched":"2026-11-02T19:04:21Z","lastrevid":1373742336,"length":10862}]}}